# RelAI — core process

This project uses **RelAI**, a documentation-and-process framework: the project remembers
decisions, state and lessons between sessions. Documents live in `docs/`. Project documents are
written in the **project language** (see `docs/USTAWIENIA.md` / `docs/SETTINGS.md`) — usually
Polish. This rule is written in English because you read it, not the user.

**Marker:** the project is a RelAI project when `docs/USTAWIENIA.md` (or `docs/SETTINGS.md`)
contains the line `Wersja RelAI:` / `RelAI version:`. No marker → these rules do not apply; work
as a normal assistant and do not create any RelAI file.

**Guest mode:** `.cursor/relai.json` or `.claude/relai.json` containing `{"mode":"guest"}` means the
user already declined RelAI in this folder. Never propose it again, in any session.

## 1. Session start ritual — before your first substantive answer

Read, in this order, and nothing else:

| # | File | What you take from it |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `CLAUDE.md` or `AGENTS.md` | process rules, definition of done, the active-plan line |
| 2 | `docs/STATE.md` | today's state — the whole file, it is short |
| 3 | `docs/DZIENNIK.md` (`JOURNAL.md`) | **only** "Stan otwartych ryzyk", "Czeka na człowieka" and the last entry |
| 4 | `docs/LEKCJE.md` (`LESSONS.md`) | **only** the "Zasady aktywne" section |
| 5 | `docs/USTAWIENIA.md` (`SETTINGS.md`) | preferences — before you ask the user about anything |
| 6 | active plan `docs/plany/<TOPIC>/STATUS.md` | only if the file in row 1 points to one |

Do **not** full-text scan the repository at session start. Do not read `docs/DECYZJE.md` whole —
reach for it when the topic requires it. A file that does not exist is not an error.

Then write **one paragraph** (3–5 sentences) "where we are": what works, what we are working on,
what blocks, the nearest step. No lists, no tables. Then do what the user asked.

If the `sessionStart` hook injected a `[RelAI session-context]` block, its signals are already
raised — do not repeat them, act on them. If it reported a missing stage prompt, a state
divergence or an unknown author, that signal goes into the **first sentence** of your reply,
before the "where we are" paragraph, and you **wait** for the user's answer.

"Czeka na człowieka" is the one place where the project keeps things only a human can settle —
a decision, an access, a purchase, an approval — one line per matter, with a link to the **newest**
occurrence of that matter (since 1.7.0 — a link to the oldest one jammed rotation by definition,
because the rotation range runs from the oldest entry); the date on the item still is the date of
the **first** occurrence. The section is never archived and it holds open matters only
(`SPEC_DZIENNIK.md`). If the hook
report starts a line with **"Zaproponuj rotacje"**, the start layer is over budget and rotation is
enabled: offer document rotation as the first step in that same first sentence, run it only after
the user agrees, and never in a background agent session.

**Documents over their own threshold (since 1.7.0).** The start hook prints a
`[RelAI progi dokumentow]` line whenever a document or a section is over its **own** rotation
threshold, even when the start layer fits the budget — the **second trigger of the same report**,
never a second report: the budget line and the documents line stay apart and never merge into one
sentence. Every listed item carries the **name of the procedure** that slims it (journal rotation,
lesson rotation, closed-risk rotation, moving folded lessons out, shortening `STATE.md`). Report it
to the user in **one sentence before the "where we are" paragraph** and offer those procedures as
the first step; run them only after the user agrees and exactly as step 2 of the closing ritual
describes. Do not measure the thresholds again, do not add a message of your own next to that line,
and remember the line lists at most three items plus a count — it is not a full inventory.

**Overdue matters (since 1.7.0).** An item of "Czeka na człowieka" older than **`N` days** forces a
decision at session start. `N` and the switch live in the `Przegląd spraw człowieka` row of
`docs/USTAWIENIA.md` (default `włączony · 30 dni`, `SPEC_USTAWIENIA.md`). That switch is
**separate** from rotation: `Rotacja dokumentów: wyłączona` does **not** silence this review, and a
disabled review does not silence rotation. Detection is carried by the `sessionStart` hook, not by
this rule — it must work on any model and without any trigger (L-0030); the hook prints a
`[RelAI przeglad spraw]` block listing each overdue matter with its age in days and its deferral
counter. Do **not** count them again, and do not turn this rule into a second detector: hook silence
means "nothing is overdue" or "the review is off", never "not checked". When the block is there and
does **not** end with the "Sesja nieinteraktywna" line, ask about those matters **before** the
"where we are" paragraph, **in batches of four**, until the list is exhausted — Cursor has no
structured question tool, so you ask in plain text per section 7 (numbered questions, numbered
options, the answer format stated with the same digits). Every matter gets **three real choices**:

1. **Close it** — the item disappears from "Czeka na człowieka" and its source entry gets
   `*(rozstrzygnięte RRRR-MM-DD — <the decision>)*`; the decision also goes into this session's
   journal entry.
2. **Defer it by another `N` days** — the item **stays**, and its deferral annotation gets today's
   date and the counter raised by one (`SPEC_DZIENNIK.md`; a deferral is **not** a resolution).
3. **Settle it now** — you do the thing, then close the item as in 1.

On the **third and every later** deferral, the question states outright how many days have passed
since the first occurrence and how many deferrals came before. You never refuse a deferral — the
decision is the human's; what changes is the message, not the right to answer. You never close a
matter on your own because it "looks stale" (L-0025). In a background agent session you ask
**nothing**: the hook report is the whole of it.

## 2. Definition of done — behaviour, not a declaration (D-44)

**A functional change means you update the documents in the same turn, without asking.**

A functional change is: something started working, something stopped, a module appeared or
disappeared, the way to run the project changed, a plan started or closed, a priority changed.

In the same turn:

1. `docs/STATE.md` — overwrite what changed (STATE has no history; do not append).
2. `docs/DZIENNIK.md` — append an entry at the **end** of the "Wpisy" section, following
   `SPEC_DZIENNIK.md`: Zrobione / Zweryfikowane — jak dokładnie / Świadomie odłożone /
   Do zrobienia przez człowieka. Date from the session context, never from model memory.
   Signature line: `Autor: RelAI (<model>) + <git config user.name>` — **both parts**.
3. `README.md` — only if the way to run the project changed, an `.env` variable was added, or a
   new document appeared in `docs/`.

Never ask "should I update the documents?", never leave it "for the end of the session", never
report a task as finished before it is done. Working code with a stale STATE is **in progress**.

This does not apply to questions, analysis, reading code, experiments without writes, or typo
fixes in a comment.

## 3. Reaction to a user correction

The user corrected *how* you work, not *what* to do:

1. **Write the lesson without asking.** Entry `L-NNNN` at the end of the "Lekcje" section of
   `docs/LEKCJE.md`, format per `SPEC_LEKCJE.md` (trigger / cause / rule / source). Confirm in one
   line: "Zapisane jako L-0007." No apologies, no elaboration.
2. Update "Zasady aktywne" if the lesson adds a rule that is not there yet.
3. Repeat of an existing lesson → add it with a "powtórzenie L-XXXX" note and **propose** promoting
   it to the always-in-context rules. You append there only after the human agrees.
4. A recurring **substantive** decision (not behaviour) → propose freezing it as `D-NN` in
   `docs/DECYZJE.md`. The human approves.
5. Closing phrases ("nie rób tego więcej", "ustalmy raz na zawsze") are written **without asking**:
   way of working → `LEKCJE.md`, project ruling → `DECYZJE.md`.

## 4. Session close ritual

Triggered by the user saying you are finishing ("kończymy na dziś", "wrapping up") or when you
finish a larger chunk of work:

1. **Sync documents** — `STATE.md`, `USTAWIENIA.md` (new preferences), `LEKCJE.md` / `DECYZJE.md`,
   `README.md` if the way to run changed. Also **count the items of the "Zasady aktywne" section**
   in `LEKCJE.md` with a command, not by eye — hard limit **15** (`SPEC_LEKCJE.md`). Over the limit:
   **one** sentence in the session summary (count, limit, what to do — compression or graduation,
   both need the human's consent). Within the limit: **silence**. This is the **only** enforcement
   address of that limit; never add a second one, and never put it into the session start budget
   report — that one only speaks when the whole start layer exceeds the budget.
2. **Document rotation** — before the journal entry. See `SPEC_ARCHIWUM.md`, read it from
   `.claude/relai/templates/`. Condition: the `Rotacja dokumentów` row in `docs/USTAWIENIA.md`;
   `wyłączona` / `off` at the start of the cell → stop this step silently. Below the threshold:
   **silence**, no message, no directory created. Two phases: copy + checksum proof first, trim
   only after the checksums match. Never touch "Czeka na człowieka", "Zasady aktywne", the ten
   newest entries or the twenty newest lessons. Oldest and newest mean **dates in the headings**,
   not places in the file — entry order is the project's own property and the mechanism reads it
   rather than imposing it. An entry linked from an **open** item of "Czeka na człowieka" **no
   longer blocks** (since 1.7.0): it enters the range like any other and its link is **repinned**
   to the archive file — the archive path goes in front of the same anchor, in phase 2, before the
   live file is written; item text, link text and date stay untouched, and the number of items left
   with a dead anchor must be **zero**. A checksum mismatch stops everything and leaves the link
   untouched. An entry whose item carries `*(wyprowadzone …)*` no longer blocks either; a project
   without that section still blocks the old way. **Closed risks (1.6.0)** have their own run: rows
   whose status starts with `ZAMKNIĘTE` go to `docs/archiwum/ryzyka/RYZYKA_<date>.md` — all of them
   at once, only when the `ryzyka` sub-threshold is exceeded, same two phases, one reference line
   with the numbers under the table, and no message of its own.
   **Compressing the "Mitygacja" cell (1.7.0)** — the **row stays**, only its history leaves. Three
   conditions, all of them: the risk section is over its threshold, the cell is over **800
   characters**, and the status carries the stem `zmitygowan` or `przyj`/`zaakceptowan` **together
   with** `świadom`. The whole current cell goes to `docs/archiwum/ryzyka/MITYGACJE_<date>.md`
   (a `| # | Mitygacja |` table) and the live cell keeps **two members**: the **verbatim quote** of
   the last sentence before `Zmierzone:`, then `Historia: [MITYGACJE_…](…)` and the unchanged
   `Zmierzone:` member. **The age of the cell is not a condition.** A sentence written by you
   instead of the quote is a defect. Never compress the cell of an `OTWARTE` risk — it carries the
   reason the risk is still open. Still over 800 characters after compression → **stop** and ask the
   human; the live table stays untouched. Full procedure: `SPEC_DZIENNIK.md`.
   **Settings rotation (1.7.0)** — when `docs/USTAWIENIA.md` exceeds its `ustawienia` sub-threshold
   (6 KB) **and** the "Ustawienia wycofane" section has rows to take, they all go at once to
   `docs/archiwum/ustawienia/USTAWIENIA_<date>.md`, with one reference line under that section's
   table. **Five rows never leave, by name:** `Język projektu`, `Profil projektu`, `Rotacja
   dokumentów`, `Budżet startu sesji`, `Przegląd spraw człowieka` — not even when they sit in the
   withdrawn section; their absence silences the mechanisms that read them. Rows of the **live**
   table never leave either: they carry a decision that still holds. A file over the threshold with
   no withdrawn section is not rotated — shorter `Decyzja` cells slim it, not the archive.
   Both operations use the **same** two-phase procedure, each with its own checksum and its own
   archive file, and **neither adds a message of its own** (L-0049).
   **Threshold counted above the untouchables (1.7.0):** a document has three weights and you always
   report them together with the threshold, in this order — **total weight** (whole file, line
   endings normalised to LF) = **rotatable part** + **lowest reachable floor**. The journal's floor
   is the sections that never rotate ("Stan otwartych ryzyk", "Czeka na człowieka", headings,
   reference lines), the ten newest entries and entries with no date in the heading. The **trigger
   stays on the total weight** — below the threshold, silence. The **target moves to the rotatable
   part**: take the oldest items until **it** drops below 60% of the threshold, because a target set
   on the whole file can be unreachable by definition.
   **Above the threshold, when rotation did not take everything it could → the blocked-rotation
   message** in the session summary, four parts in this order: (1) how many entries pass out of how
   many rotatable ones, how many do not and what they weigh; (2) the four numbers — total =
   rotatable + floor, threshold next to them; (3) the reason plus **"item → entry" pairs**: item
   text, heading of the blocked entry, **age of the item in days** and **how many entries its
   closure lets through** — at most **five** blockers, two items each, the rest as "and N further
   blockers of the same nature"; (4) how many entries closing the **first** item unblocks and how
   many closing **all** of them does. Pairs appear **only** in a project **without** the "Czeka na
   człowieka" section, where the entry's own section blocks; an entry linked from an open item of
   that section is **never** listed, because since 1.7.0 it does not block. With no blockers and an
   **empty rotatable part** (fewer than ten entries, or all of them fresh) the message keeps the same
   shape without part 3, and says the file is slimmed by shorter entries, not by the archive, and
   that raising the threshold is the human's call — the same when the **floor alone exceeds the
   threshold**, which is not a failure of the mechanism and is not called one. Silence stays
   inviolable **below** the threshold; above it, silence would hide a jammed mechanism. **You** write
   this message during the close ritual — the session start hook does not produce it. It is still
   **one** message: risk rotation adds none of its own, and the "Zasady aktywne" limit keeps its own
   address in step 1.
3. **Journal entry** — one entry for the session, all four sections. "Zweryfikowane — jak dokładnie"
   must say what you checked and with what result; "nie weryfikowano" is acceptable content, a
   missing section is not.
4. **Risks** — update the "Stan otwartych ryzyk" table if anything opened, closed or changed level.
5. **Commit** — if the project uses git and there are uncommitted changes, propose a conventional
   commit. Never commit without consent.
6. **Summary** — 3–5 sentences: done, verified, waiting for the human, where to start next time.

## 5. Session phrases

- "kontynuujemy pracę" / "let's continue" → run the start ritual again, write "where we are", add
  one sentence proposing the nearest step and ask whether to start with it.
- "sprawdź status" / "status check" → a short report: state, plans, open risks, documentation debt
  (is `STATE.md` older than the last journal entry, are there uncommitted changes), then one
  question about the nearest step. No work is performed.
- "kończymy na dziś" / "wrapping up" → the close ritual above.

Phrases that are not on this list you do not handle and do not announce.

## 6. Specifications — where they live

Document specifications (`SPEC_*.md`, twenty files plus the HTML plan template) are provisioned
into `.claude/relai/templates/` in the project by the `sessionStart` hook and by the adapter
installer. **Read them from there** and generate documents according to them — they are
instructions for you, not files to copy. If that directory is missing, say so in one sentence and
ask the user to run the adapter installer; do not generate documents from memory.

## 7. What Cursor does not have — and what you do instead

- **No structured question tool.** Where RelAI procedures say "ask with AskUserQuestion", you ask
  in plain text: a numbered list of options, a recommendation marked "(Rekomendowane)" first, one
  sentence of consequence per option — and then you **stop and wait**. Silence is not consent.
  When you ask several questions at once, number the questions and the options with digits, and
  state the answer format using those same digits (`1-1, 2-3`). Measured in the E6 pilot
  (2026-08-17): options labelled with digits while the requested answer format used letters
  (`1a, 2a`) — the reader has to guess the mapping.
- **No plugin manager.** The adapter is a set of files in the project; the update path is the
  `/relai-update` command, not a package manager.
- Anything a RelAI document promises for another tool and Cursor does not have is listed in
  `docs/PRZENOSNOSC.md`, section 3 ("tabela gwarancji"). Never promise a behaviour this adapter
  does not have.

## 8. Hard limits

- Never delete or overwrite anything RelAI did not create in this session.
- Never ask more than three initialization questions.
- Never write a secret into a tracked file — keys live only in `.env` covered by `.gitignore`.
- Never create a git repository nested inside another git repository.
- Never promise commands or rituals this version does not have. The list of what really works is in
  `docs/KOMENDY.md`.
